# Text

## Paragraphs {#paragraphs}

Text is written in paragraphs, separated by blank lines. The lines of a paragraph are joined: break them where it suits the source.

:example
```
A paragraph, written
on three lines of
the source.

Another paragraph.
```

## Styles {#styles}

:example
```
**bold**, *italic*, __underlined__,
==highlighted== and ~~struck~~.

Styles nest: ***bold and italic***,
**bold with *italic* inside**.
```

A style's markers are around its text, in the same paragraph: `*` opens italic, and the next `*` closes it. A marker that is not closed is an error, so a `*` meant as a star is escaped.

## Escapes {#escapes}

A backslash makes the character after it plain text: `\*`, `\[`, `\$`, `\%`, `\\`.

:example
```
\*not italic\*, a price of \$5,
\[brackets], and a backslash: \\.
```

## Comments {#comments}

`::` starts a comment, to the end of the line, at the start of a line or after a space. Comments are not part of the page.

:example
```
Shown. :: not shown
:: not shown either
Shown too.
```

## Inline code {#inline-code}

Code in a line is between backticks, shown as written. Around code that holds backticks, use longer runs of them.

:example
```
`let x = 1;`, and ``a `tick` inside``.
```

`:code` highlights it in a language, named as for [[code#code-block|code blocks]]:

:example
```
:code[rust]`let x: u32 = 1;`
```

## Links {#links}

:example
```
[NML on GitHub](https://github.com/ef3d0c3e/nml),
and a bare one: [](https://example.org).
```

A link without text shows its URL. Links are for URLs; to point to a heading, a figure or another page of the workspace, use a [[references#|reference]], which follows its target when it moves.

## Line breaks and rules

There is no syntax for a line break inside a paragraph, nor for a horizontal rule. [[blocks#raw|Raw HTML]] gives both: ``:raw`<br>` ``, and a `:raw` block with `<hr>`.
